#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221169 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "6649e376-1865-5cb9-8892-687f80082d22" :question/math-academy-id "e-21650" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=-5,$ with corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [3, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-2, \\ 1]^T,$ respectively. Using this information, sketch the phase portrait of the system." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n![](images/b6/b6e1ba9b197e105bfa8599f81ef6a15381cf2d996f75528733b8bf5f32ed1f66.svg)\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ![](images/c8/c83c3bdb26e609cf9e5d023ea9e4f4bf366162dc9957972355b0eaab06c42a44.svg) are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c39f92243806a8682701374244fa2f7def32ab56ae509c920a31e75abfacb88f.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/bf/bf953962efa5f9c6f59878328bd99be9f795d31e05069e8925b520a48eedc3c7.svg) has the equation \n$y=- \\dfrac12x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/0e/0eec91967012c45aadd7ed416606e7ff0f5d9fdbfe7a9a3b75a24108a6160d00.svg)\nlie on that line. Since $e^{-5t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/96/96a198c7fe3d98d3775ffc176b6659022b18676caf849cbc8d5c66833113bb19.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/74/74b573da705aafba25c74aa789dc3a51464683dd5a47a999ba9c557d35ed1d02.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221170 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "e89c64cb-e046-592c-929b-9269cb7b2758" :question/math-academy-id "e-21651" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = [3, \\ 1]^T.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [-1, \\ 0]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$  Sketch the phase portrait of the system." :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](images/b0/b0b5abdfe48d45a352b101e134ebb06165824385c1162a705e539f911af9f267.svg) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3t-1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/96/962887c2e859c2afadfc122bd5ecc22fe28eb354b348c5153c16577de5fe37d8.svg) are equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfc7b00f7f63aec120179da3f89f72f0aa53f13d299c95ed8f53e6238d329e51.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n![](images/9b/9bbb4470407c8498cf3270e247e7b9a9d214bd1ff1b3d85a75d17189bee26ea9.svg)\nFor example:\n\n- When $c_1=1$ and $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3t+2 \\\\ y &=t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/55/55dfdfce480deba74fe46c1634e4c7ffb711fbd617525cdb5387bdabfc83ae75.png)\n\n- When $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3t-2 \\\\ y &=-t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/2f/2fb2297c741ea20ca729dcacb9934d7f8d1cca58af93c2b75db177890934b2b6.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e7970ba2d6bbe4745ee498cdbc72f2b7d8689219291847644bd6483696cc456.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221171 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "edb1633f-44e1-5324-8048-80252c65afe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21654" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=5x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/9d/9d8df402a06960cd5f2e0c772a5fab345e4610cc90c7e88202dab34f2ab97e65.svg)\nwhere ![](images/58/58ab8470c661bcc5f0c95df2640185f95fc9733c1f4bf718ce51dba7a0b9a989.svg)\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+2y &=0 \\\\ 5x+5y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+2y=0\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n2y=-2x\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n![](images/b9/b9059ea5506ed372cb5b21771b177966be2c607934164b2ad16466a31d423834.svg)\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8566dc89-42f7-5739-a0d1-347500de824f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/7705e0976e796f3a5642d68287dcfef5315c6599f7e997b56a34ec534b89e76d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220358} {:answer/id #uuid "a420fc82-2339-5078-a8e1-59c0d0d1479d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb3187e3229b7fc09f530035d1dffe0e8690e8056502ecb27fcb41c274b6b218.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220359} {:answer/id #uuid "b08617a8-44cd-5d28-8f4c-b9a7e2205dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/568be19a1553bf837519d82e11f8ee391c7dce120605fc2855039eeff83ff1fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220360} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5769a2-ffdc-577f-8af7-36465be42670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220361} {:answer/id #uuid "5d388a75-c148-5fe9-8996-42f5a80dec0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e0/e0bf57ef5856cebd4b50666cb508039e26796d0f41fe4848cfa50e049f010c6f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a5769a2-ffdc-577f-8af7-36465be42670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220361} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b61da0a-185b-51be-b559-d0e6be5de345" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48a05022-07e1-5409-ab9b-332660c73fa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332874" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/309cbea2fa350c44c7a9225ec046b827113f9ffaa1dea6d746ab097e66cf945a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+2 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d6/d6ec97d8fc3afffe41dca1dfd9d1918de586220408584183ef2eaad52e502485.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-2 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/09/0990ae7edeef543034fad206797305c41378b8243f809f5914f67c1e3b3955aa.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cea3552-0e9d-56f2-9e24-8d9c17e4c552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546274} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf94921-2c23-5142-b223-bc58612d9c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546346} {:answer/id #uuid "39ea73ba-1b0e-5076-8b87-cee93c2b2423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546374} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba40f14-7f52-5d96-a933-5219eeaaffc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546525} {:answer/id #uuid "b17a8d12-de07-5930-9d74-4a3fab5e331c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b17a8d12-de07-5930-9d74-4a3fab5e331c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546647} :answer-field/id #uuid "a20c8207-9935-5207-8526-d46b30276bd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e31d1b1-01e3-5911-a0b2-09271e054bd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-332876" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ 2x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d3660e-11f9-54c8-85b6-6ec7ef18c7ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\text{ only}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429947} {:answer/id #uuid "0ceddc0a-1385-5226-bad7-64fd528d2bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429948} {:answer/id #uuid "2281703b-64cd-5882-ac83-002647f8b3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3k \\\\ -6k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429950} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2e036f-6092-5f13-88cc-76a83386c5e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\text{ only}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429952} {:answer/id #uuid "e832ba8e-38d6-539c-9e5d-09409711b42c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2k \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e832ba8e-38d6-539c-9e5d-09409711b42c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2k \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429955} :answer-field/id #uuid "f226cae3-dc9e-52a4-84a0-4560715ab839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b59bb2fc-6ce1-5b76-9a55-8b95129f4121" :question/math-academy-id "q-333779" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-6y &=0 \\\\ x-2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first equation is a multiple of the second one:\n\n$$\n3(x-2y)=3x-6y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the first equation.\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2y=0\\,⇒\\,x=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2k \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a57c6e7-8e72-599e-ad3d-ed9358a413a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{ only}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546377} {:answer/id #uuid "49177e3b-f7f0-51e3-8d6c-309151a7a3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\text{ only}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546415} {:answer/id #uuid "85366ebe-6a03-5f07-b1d9-474207bf3100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3k \\\\ k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546545} {:answer/id #uuid "de354c56-0518-51d2-b8f0-a2f8152c222c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546742} {:answer/id #uuid "edf58337-b2c0-5f20-a4ee-a86272054364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edf58337-b2c0-5f20-a4ee-a86272054364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546786} :answer-field/id #uuid "be292a4e-e0bf-5c61-a277-07e6b41b4f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00d15146-6fbb-5275-8798-98c048c587e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-333780" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+y=0 \\\\ -6x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(-3x+y)=-6x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-3x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d598311-7111-5a19-a291-520640546016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546349} {:answer/id #uuid "b095957d-b0a0-555d-9d97-68a095833e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{ only}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546645} {:answer/id #uuid "be528247-6daa-5d39-99ee-bef6c29e0761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\text{ only}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546678} {:answer/id #uuid "d495a94f-0573-55e9-8ec9-1e2826693bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546722} {:answer/id #uuid "f366ce2e-67dc-5a96-b262-45fa13b6de33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d495a94f-0573-55e9-8ec9-1e2826693bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546722} :answer-field/id #uuid "6768b380-738a-5723-a58c-cf6e6b14e43c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b08d2a40-8148-5020-a105-36a37de12f17" :question/math-academy-id "q-333787" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 3x+6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n3(x+2y)=3x+6y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2y=0\\,⇒\\,x=-2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a79227e-3f05-51b3-b65f-f9c72a3b08d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305706} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6295a6-ec4e-52b3-a377-0c0db01537b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305902} {:answer/id #uuid "bef4aad5-ceb8-55d2-993b-f95f6d96f74f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306931} {:answer/id #uuid "d51b7fc6-4b10-50bf-b9ec-28ccc48da1ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307129} {:answer/id #uuid "f905c634-4fbe-5605-adac-a50b2a70878f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a79227e-3f05-51b3-b65f-f9c72a3b08d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305706} :answer-field/id #uuid "c70dc4fa-58db-5007-806c-758410caf010" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "823bb65d-36ce-5de4-8512-ee5806601897" :question/math-academy-id "q-333795" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=8x(t)-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y &=0 \\\\ 8x-4y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=2x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fed07ddb-975d-5a6f-9b33-90b64c27d3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb491075c2fecf4aa28f4b167e91ac5a0b7475cf57c1b43eb94e8e1427a28922.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391288} {:answer/id #uuid "a26dae11-7922-5cde-a25b-9e2a34d335e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6fcc3957699640731ddae9ccbb98e958882d8bdbb2f2b506112baa3553b03f47.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391289} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f8982e-0372-594a-a7c8-2ba5fa1945b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a7/a76320ebfd18b1bc272c5421309a4a98c34abc5437c56c6d40a83a84f80f0157.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391290} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c091f7-e94d-5c27-8029-5ce245439c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dd/dd3667299cd5da8fe5d9450799fbe865e832449ac39427795a5b87cf2786f587.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391291} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c7f702-bdf9-57d9-9b22-fdeeb2287606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f7784ccd8bbfe05ce743e71f92cfbecfa4d4a8b19d0664337d2c67d360b3d3e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fed07ddb-975d-5a6f-9b33-90b64c27d3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb491075c2fecf4aa28f4b167e91ac5a0b7475cf57c1b43eb94e8e1427a28922.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391288} :answer-field/id #uuid "458a647e-6339-5a42-879a-e69d51bbf669" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c13a40a3-bc1a-5171-a2d7-22e438148666" :question/math-academy-id "q-333814" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/99/99629c2f73e3a10db2ef139848c93c6a2489d995d3d992b7dfb0332989b4f48f.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/cb/cb491075c2fecf4aa28f4b167e91ac5a0b7475cf57c1b43eb94e8e1427a28922.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebc325f8-243b-5130-a053-df4211d94d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391295} {:answer/id #uuid "d13587aa-4415-592d-b84a-ac9148da1da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391296} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d7bfd8-aa3b-5bf2-b12f-bd4b4c6876d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8c699c5e49f42ddf1d57f3686f7fa7c06ca3f941aa8e65f7700f855b0134df13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391297} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ccc559-2cfe-5ecb-9b80-8f02e4bf3705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/06b9fd74afcc99a3396aa280b248272904c495d6181e6a5c027c0cd6a30acbfb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391298} {:answer/id #uuid "3919ca1c-1c68-55f4-b720-13f63a99bffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a695d58031c47e43d0aed186e271eff7d3da271ea83ae3c053be011afe9d4707.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d13587aa-4415-592d-b84a-ac9148da1da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391296} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e9d729b-0d66-5bca-bdc1-c82610c1e6ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ec66512-f01a-54c5-9b43-60952356c9b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-333824" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=6x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b3/b39e589968babd6f1417b37dc0f53796ab363ee80ec5fc3860e9793b88b1e98c.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/12/12a1f8f11c3f1063804220884e6616ae4c9e9aee956adc575de1f0e88e0dd038.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows: \n\n![](images/1b/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05542e74-e525-5cb7-8e46-a744cdb6e492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305199} {:answer/id #uuid "a28748a2-30b9-5993-b947-2209f567754d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dd/dd3667299cd5da8fe5d9450799fbe865e832449ac39427795a5b87cf2786f587.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306678} {:answer/id #uuid "a46b67c1-b13d-5ae8-957a-a664665034d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a7b634d05a938d9b481620da9837a8f28dc28f84ac18a3f9a20678ead0df20b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306700} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2d227a-1ddc-5b0b-82ec-39ad7fba9c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f4b88938a280acb1149be29d6832483713437236424bbb640784a55bafea712c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306758} {:answer/id #uuid "da4b3bdf-1f79-5acb-9f40-db982a1bfa54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59b96e2396db2559e9893c73ef530a312a347442b9c4fde0137583310ceba0a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05542e74-e525-5cb7-8e46-a744cdb6e492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305199} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b46cd2e-0392-50d7-bcaf-b0944ec48370" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b9dc234-d83d-52ed-858a-43cf39227d57" :question/math-academy-id "q-333832" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=-3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/2b/2bfda58fee727ccdc9f424140e2b8143bf5afe426ef0b1fa72105272d9a1b41e.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e45555c-a01b-528d-b0e7-37a8317e6e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546277} {:answer/id #uuid "63afdf4d-d518-5279-ab6d-da361838db26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a9/a987e981e719b1c392bd6d08f51003a9d6b6a606d8acd9d5d2e8b663bb82913d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546469} {:answer/id #uuid "b07d9674-d92c-5274-8a3c-1ffed17ea005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ffb0ea111ac2eeef013adafd1637917f48f24db5d1f7481fd1d3518160e446b8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546644} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a77373-8000-5097-b4f2-b11ecf781eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d59b96eda59c5949124cb94545f130c2dccf1a35510beff03860be1c3e314b59.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546653} {:answer/id #uuid "c5dccf30-e03b-5d05-b418-3d436cf40ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/86/868becabde44fa1173fcf0a41424bfcad678a03f5449f62706a7f814563347dc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5dccf30-e03b-5d05-b418-3d436cf40ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/86/868becabde44fa1173fcf0a41424bfcad678a03f5449f62706a7f814563347dc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546691} :answer-field/id #uuid "09c63b9d-39f4-5648-baef-f9e903889acb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ce7a74d-231a-58e8-b9b6-91d4b9871cc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-333907" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/03/033fb4b6f35c06e8d718df9b8e88d47b7461f247121f1e859c6fba41cfda9112.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=-2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/ea/eabc6882089c4e26e497b8adae004018e132874dd9e02d33caeddd3967330c87.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/86/868becabde44fa1173fcf0a41424bfcad678a03f5449f62706a7f814563347dc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f20628-065b-591f-be75-7e55983b7906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546324} {:answer/id #uuid "25299cd1-0352-507d-8e17-178efec11c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/06b9fd74afcc99a3396aa280b248272904c495d6181e6a5c027c0cd6a30acbfb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546332} {:answer/id #uuid "61ed4469-be66-5e2b-a731-509c3804a57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/16d673a4829b0c79787b4d5604a9cf9c7b5c025426bede9cd9ef6cffdc059b3a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546461} {:answer/id #uuid "958574b7-28d2-52ee-bddc-23ae0634e442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a695d58031c47e43d0aed186e271eff7d3da271ea83ae3c053be011afe9d4707.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546580} {:answer/id #uuid "a1bc3c22-7580-5a23-89ea-55fe06e0f7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8c699c5e49f42ddf1d57f3686f7fa7c06ca3f941aa8e65f7700f855b0134df13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1bc3c22-7580-5a23-89ea-55fe06e0f7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8c699c5e49f42ddf1d57f3686f7fa7c06ca3f941aa8e65f7700f855b0134df13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546612} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab119a6d-ecae-5c97-9ab8-0bc9b74391d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33c6aa75-206a-500b-acd6-afa11d420835" :question/math-academy-id "q-333980" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fde17e6b52cb472d517caee4fcb84e9aee9465ebd391f482316b487486ebe6e0.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/5d/5d95c4d8ab87366960b99dbbcd898214e2ee883da7ceb1926d44968c204d2497.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/8c/8c699c5e49f42ddf1d57f3686f7fa7c06ca3f941aa8e65f7700f855b0134df13.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2916d28-5efb-5314-8923-0573bec1151e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1d/1dca375ebe6f8468f12a1729d697d8d35ff6b430a70e53639ab029254ac392fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391482} {:answer/id #uuid "ea635c52-25f9-5aa9-95df-3db5bd2c8c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/87/879cc00fedd506a0607bce3d3d36f99b489f599dadb6b13d4d1176fa24441853.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391483} {:answer/id #uuid "1757ec4c-4154-59ad-82df-49a519e58859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3f80cbae958772e0fa0492f5c426d412f002c3baa41a8a69b38e594512823422.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391484} {:answer/id #uuid "20b254ca-ca7c-577b-af73-4554ed77ae3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c9a56a6a1f17d58226fb2fac905247b339be111335637a15d3e8c447aab3f3a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391485} {:answer/id #uuid "de04ae64-037b-57a5-a073-94201161c12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a178460d4e87756694e03bc37dd58c3e70cf3222389b31fe838aeb6a910b984.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1757ec4c-4154-59ad-82df-49a519e58859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3f80cbae958772e0fa0492f5c426d412f002c3baa41a8a69b38e594512823422.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391484} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b27342d-f535-5330-acd6-ae18222b5178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb0146a-edb8-5224-b29c-36d6ad8aec81" :question/math-academy-id "q-334532" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-2x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c3afc4bedb411c778773190a3b9bc1db0b376dd69592ba744e8225ec900cc45.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-t-1 \\\\ y &=2t+3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/0d/0d506993d3bfbb727dffd44ced1a588b40e2fefa86e7f2780d90277c9d0a9eed.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t+1 \\\\ y &=-2t-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/e8/e88dfa02c57a5c31df14fdf6f963f7d568eaebccbbbfba6d8f2958593ac4693a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/3f/3f80cbae958772e0fa0492f5c426d412f002c3baa41a8a69b38e594512823422.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6924d9b1-5a1b-5960-9047-6dace2c0f5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306154} {:answer/id #uuid "814d39f6-0bc8-5bc2-8c52-0b58cbdd6a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306385} {:answer/id #uuid "dc83e9d5-26db-5557-a16f-a01467cfa2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57684286e917c2dcbcb6f6a7f19a82d5603c1fe11a39cb8a6b12473cce5105f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307192} {:answer/id #uuid "e85efaf2-e739-544b-9816-5781974d8412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307296} {:answer/id #uuid "f08f2cc8-1389-5ef3-8d98-74db52713374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c999c1938803ff00ff445cdeb063f679e06acf47109cbcba01eeb6ed502e3d33.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc83e9d5-26db-5557-a16f-a01467cfa2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57684286e917c2dcbcb6f6a7f19a82d5603c1fe11a39cb8a6b12473cce5105f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307192} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a67073-c266-558d-9507-3b272eced88b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22d70586-a868-5b19-aa40-b97ef9910f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334620" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5afce1bca483dbd9bd462810c351d710469b744813cd9f54ea3234f0c7343d35.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+1 \\\\ y &=t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/f7/f7be54fdf4b1cbe65be9730989c06b934604bc8f0b4300bd6b616ece9de7b8f5.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2t-1 \\\\ y &=-t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/47/47c41f2d62aea217d56259bf9918b1eba5bed3141d2bcfcfd486241bc32df048.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/57/57684286e917c2dcbcb6f6a7f19a82d5603c1fe11a39cb8a6b12473cce5105f5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fecab66-dba7-588d-b051-c89c33672842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305295} {:answer/id #uuid "4c93c30d-56ef-5dbb-a2c3-12f0c02b1cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305891} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0da9f4-1a35-5132-acee-bff9e95cc05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4a8d9528c878089370476a45aab127a416a11595ea0b0f972708e5161f208d5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306355} {:answer/id #uuid "8a151970-06b2-5c13-989f-71d57aa7a478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306471} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1b08d6-6709-5e77-8f04-4e1eb9a5d4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fecab66-dba7-588d-b051-c89c33672842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305295} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4c7528-85a6-56a0-a678-684c0edfdcb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fbf3cd8-344d-52ae-9b3c-567d393e20b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334638" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-9x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }3]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[-\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a70188cf5de7e7b64530aa661447b7fa7fc1e918ca3de41088e67274d813b7a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3t \\\\ y &=9t+3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/37/37deb5a0fda7ef15a016ec2d73a3b935074b6696184dd480ebe5b858ce517500.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3t \\\\ y &=-9t-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/60/60b49680cf94888122f25432586a3546dea206ae960f951afd187444afc8881a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee5b833-9c64-5e6b-aab0-da9f11cd3451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/19/19f9668a913eb5d108bf1043d23ba277035f619eca48f6d428856a5ba77b981b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305448} {:answer/id #uuid "3b15c480-edfe-542f-8483-6b2833fe4565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305713} {:answer/id #uuid "590a2be6-20b9-5f8b-abdb-aa47cd31c158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306006} {:answer/id #uuid "daf1a0fb-c311-5d2c-bdd2-fb2a1cba88cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307178} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f4c08b-8d94-52a5-9594-a17424d77fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9f4c08b-8d94-52a5-9594-a17424d77fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307467} :answer-field/id #uuid "36c73a9c-a0d1-5afe-aba6-629aee1682ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7a8620e-ad1d-5894-b095-0459bbd949b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-334641" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)-8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[\\frac{1}{2},0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bff5cf9d21f0b01571b577ebe3d98ef08525d13a70a1d7379b537f6ba5319613.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4t+3 \\\\ y &=2t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/7d/7d590ee38f7f1b6b43c9b650890f42fa31b4c15951dab8bcb003b1ac1cc45c39.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-4t-3 \\\\ y &=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/b9/b965e3e244bc49b5e0f52840550c2ae997a0e38dc40e8757f0f51905c448d7af.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png)"}]}
